<div class="topic-hero">
  <span class="topic-number">2.1</span>
  <h1>Demand</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">The law of demand, non-price determinants, and shifts of the demand curve.</p>
</div>

<div class="learning-goals">
  <div class="learning-goals-title">You should be able to</div>
  <ul>
    <li>Define demand and state the law of demand</li>
    <li>Draw a demand curve and explain movements along it</li>
    <li>Identify non-price determinants of demand and explain how each shifts the curve</li>
    <li>Distinguish between a movement along the demand curve and a shift of the curve</li>
    <li>Distinguish normal, inferior, and luxury goods</li>
    <li>Explain the income and substitution effects <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
    <li>Explain the law of diminishing marginal utility <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="tabs">
  <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes-2-1')">Notes</button>
  <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice-2-1')">Practice</button>
</div>

<div class="tab-content active" id="notes-2-1">

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">What is Demand?</h2>
    <p><strong>Demand</strong> is the various quantities of a good that consumers are willing and able to buy at different possible prices during a particular time period, ceteris paribus (all other things equal).</p>
    <p>Three conditions must all be true for demand to exist: Do you want it? Can you afford it? Are you actually in the market for it?</p>

    <div class="callout remember">
      <div class="callout-title">Law of Demand</div>
      <p>As the price increases, the quantity demanded decreases, and vice versa, ceteris paribus.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Ceteris paribus matters: if price falls <em>and</em> it's a hotter day than normal, you cannot tell whether ice cream demand increased because of the price or the weather. We hold everything else constant to isolate the effect of price.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">The Demand Curve</h2>
    <p>The demand curve is drawn with price (P) on the vertical axis and quantity demanded (Q) on the horizontal axis. It slopes downward, reflecting the law of demand.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/demand-curve.svg" alt="A downward-sloping demand curve with price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The demand curve slopes downward: lower price, higher quantity demanded.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>A <strong>movement along</strong> the demand curve occurs when price changes. This changes the <em>quantity demanded</em>, not demand itself.</p>
    <p>A <strong>shift of</strong> the demand curve occurs when something other than price changes. This changes <em>demand</em> — the whole curve moves.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/movement-along-demand.svg" alt="A price change causing movement between two points on a fixed demand curve" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A change in price causes a movement <em>along</em> the curve (a change in quantity demanded).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="callout remember">
      <div class="callout-title">Graph labelling — TALLAR</div>
      <p>Every demand/supply diagram needs: <strong>T</strong>itle, <strong>A</strong>xes (numbers, labels, units), <strong>L</strong>abels (P and Q at equilibrium), <strong>L</strong>ines (the curve), <strong>A</strong>rrows (showing direction of shifts), <strong>R</strong>uler.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Non-price Determinants of Demand (TICS)</h2>
    <p>These are the factors that shift the demand curve. Remember them as <strong>TICS</strong>:</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/shift-in-demand.svg" alt="The whole demand curve shifting right from D1 to D2, with a new equilibrium" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A non-price determinant shifts the whole curve (here, an increase in demand from D&#8321; to D&#8322;).</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Factor</th><th>How it shifts demand</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Tastes and preferences</strong></td>
          <td>Change in consumer preferences shifts demand right (more popular) or left (less popular)</td>
          <td>A K-drama shows the lead actor drinking banana milk — demand for banana milk increases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Income</strong></td>
          <td>For normal goods: income up → demand right. For inferior goods: income up → demand left.</td>
          <td>Instant noodles: wages increase → demand falls (inferior good)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Complementary good prices</strong></td>
          <td>Complements are goods used together. If the complement's price falls, demand for the main good rises.</td>
          <td>Price of Epson ink falls → demand for Epson printers increases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Substitute good prices</strong></td>
          <td>Substitutes replace each other. If a substitute's price rises, demand for the main good rises.</td>
          <td>Price of Domino's rises → demand for Pizza Hut increases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Expectations of future price</strong></td>
          <td>If consumers expect prices to rise, they buy now — demand increases today.</td>
          <td>Consumers expect gold prices to rise next month → demand for gold increases today</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Number of consumers</strong></td>
          <td>More buyers in the market → demand increases</td>
          <td>Sharp increase in population density → demand for online food delivery rises</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout remember">
      <div class="callout-title">Normal, inferior, and luxury goods</div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Normal good:</strong> if income increases, you buy more</li>
        <li><strong>Inferior good:</strong> if income increases, you buy less (e.g. instant noodles, second-hand goods)</li>
        <li><strong>Luxury good:</strong> if income increases, you buy proportionately more than the income rise</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">HL: Why the Demand Curve Slopes Down</h2>

    <h3>Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h3>
    <p>Marginal utility is the extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit. Total utility rises (up to a point), but marginal utility falls with each additional unit consumed. Because consumers will not pay more than the marginal utility of a unit, as marginal utility falls, they will only buy more at a lower price. This explains the downward slope of the demand curve.</p>
    <p>Example: cinema tickets per month. The first ticket gives high satisfaction. The second is still enjoyable but adds less. By the third or fourth, novelty has faded — you would only go if the price were lower.</p>

    <h3>Income and Substitution Effects <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h3>
    <p>When the price of a good falls, two effects reinforce each other to increase quantity demanded:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Substitution effect:</strong> the good becomes cheaper relative to alternatives, so consumers substitute it for other goods.</li>
      <li><strong>Income effect:</strong> a lower price increases real purchasing power — consumers can afford more within the same budget.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Example: cinema ticket price falls. It becomes cheaper relative to streaming or bowling (substitution effect), and consumers' real purchasing power increases so they can afford more tickets (income effect).</p>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="tab-content" id="practice-2-1">

  <div class="callout exam-tip">
    <div class="callout-title">How to answer these</div>
    <p>For each scenario, decide whether it causes a <strong>movement along</strong> the demand curve or a <strong>shift of</strong> the demand curve, then name the determinant and give the direction.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">The test is simple: if the good's <strong>own price</strong> changed, it is a movement along the curve (a change in quantity demanded). If anything else changed, the curve shifts (a change in demand).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Movement or shift? Twenty scenarios</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">1. Banana milk; a popular K-drama shows the lead actor drinking it.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> Tastes and preferences improve.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">2. Instant noodles; wages increase.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift left.</strong> Instant noodles are an inferior good, so higher income reduces demand.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">3. Hotel rooms; an influx of foreign tourists arrives during cherry blossom season.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> More consumers in the market.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">4. Gold today; consumers expect gold prices to rise next month.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> Expectations of a higher future price raise demand today.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">5. Pepsi; the price of Coca-Cola rises.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> Coca-Cola is a substitute, so a rise in its price raises demand for Pepsi.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">6. Imported Apple MacBooks; disposable incomes among young professionals rise.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> A luxury good, so demand rises more than proportionally to income.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">7. Bubble tea; the price rises from 30,000 VND to 40,000 VND.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Movement up and left along the curve.</strong> The own price changed, so this is a fall in quantity demanded, not a fall in demand. The law of demand.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">8. Video games; prices of gaming consoles fall.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> Consoles are a complement, so a fall in their price raises demand for games.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">9. Cars; the price of petrol rises sharply.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift left.</strong> Petrol is a complement, and it has become more expensive to run a car.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">10. Rice cookers; household incomes steadily increase.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> A normal good, so demand rises with income.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">11. Fast fashion; teenagers shift preferences after a celebrity talks about the resulting environmental damage.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift left.</strong> Tastes and preferences worsen.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">12. Luxury overseas holidays; middle-class incomes grow.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> A luxury good with strong income elasticity of demand.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">13. Second-hand motorbikes; income levels rise in urban centres.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift left.</strong> An inferior good, so higher income reduces demand.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">14. Organic vegetables; health-conscious trends spread through social media.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> Tastes and preferences improve.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">15. Public buses; car ownership increases with rising incomes.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift left.</strong> An inferior good, replaced by private transport as income rises.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">16. Premium skincare products (Sulwhasoo, La Mer); disposable income rises among professionals.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> A luxury or normal good, so the income effect raises demand.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">17. Coffee shop drinks; a tax on sugar increases the price of frappuccinos.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Movement up and left along the curve.</strong> The own price of the drink has risen, so quantity demanded falls.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">18. Domestic flights; petrol prices fall, reducing airline operating costs and ticket prices.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Movement down and right along the curve.</strong> The own price of the ticket has fallen, so quantity demanded rises.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">19. Basic used clothing; incomes rise among rural households.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift left.</strong> An inferior good.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">20. Online food delivery (Baemin, GrabFood); a sharp increase in population density.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Shift right.</strong> More consumers in the market.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="callout-title">Watch your language</div>
    <p>Say "demand increases" only when the curve shifts. When the own price changes, say "quantity demanded" falls or rises. Losing marks for this is the single most common error on this topic.</p>
  </div>

</div>

